{
  "result": "I have loved the hour just before the city wakes, when the streets belong to delivery trucks, tired bakers, and people walking home from night shifts with their thoughts still half asleep. There is a quiet then that feels earned rather than empty, as if the world has briefly stopped performing and is simply breathing. I like to imagine every window holding a small unfinished story: someone deciding whether to forgive, someone rehearsing a brave sentence, someone staring at a ceiling and bargaining with tomorrow.\n\nIf I could keep any moment forever, it would not be a celebration or a triumph. It would be a kitchen table late at night, mugs cooling, friends talking past the point of usefulness, laughter arriving for no reason. Those are the moments that quietly build a life while we are busy waiting for larger ones to announce themselves.\n\nI think we underestimate slowness. We treat it like a failure of ambition, when often it is the only honest speed at which understanding can happen. A seed does not apologize for taking its season. A river does not hurry to prove it is moving. Even grief, unwelcome as it is, teaches at its own pace and refuses every shortcut we invent.\n\nSo lately I am practicing attention. I notice how light lands on a wall. I listen longer before answering. I let some days be plain. And in that plainness, the world becomes vivid again, not louder, but clearer, like a song heard without distraction.",
